readings.c3s1:
¶
'''tufty'''<sup>1</sup> (tuf′ti), ''a.'' [< ''tuft''<sup>1</sup> + ''-y''<sup>1</sup>.] Abounding in tufts; wooded.
{{smaller block|The sylvans . . . about the neighbouring woods did dwell,<br>Both in the ''tufty'' frith and in the mossy fell.
{{right|''Drayton'', Polyolbion, xvii. 387.}}}}

'''tufty'''<sup>2</sup> (tuf′ti), ''a.'' [< ''tuft''<sup>2</sup> + ''-y''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' Abounding in tufts or knots.
{{smaller block|Here the ground lay jagged and shaggy, wrought up with high tufts of reed, . . . this ''tufty'', flaggy ground.
{{right|''R. D. Blackmore'', Lorna Doone, lix.}}}}
'''2.''' Growing in tufts.
{{smaller block|Where ''tufty'' daisies nod at every gale.
{{right|''W. Browne'', Britannia’s Pastorals, i. 5.}}}}

'''tug''' (tug), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''tugged'', ppr. ''tugging''. [< ME. ''tuggen'', ''toggen'', ''togen'', a secondary form of ''tukken'', pull: see ''tuck''<sup>1</sup>, ''tow''<sup>1</sup>, ''tee''<sup>1</sup>.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To pull or draw with sturdy effort or violent strain; haul with force; pull.
{{smaller block|<poem>''Togyd'' with tene [sorrow] was god of prys;
To don hym sorwe was here delys [their delight];
He seyde no word loth.</poem>
{{right|''Political Poems'', etc. (ed. Furnivall), p. 248.}}}}
{{smaller block|Turkes slauish ''tugging'' oares.
{{right|''Dekker'', Londons Tempe (Works, ed. Pearson, IV. 119).}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>As when a slaughter’d bull’s yet-reeking hide,
Strain’d with full force, and ''tugg’d'' from side to side,
The brawny curriers stretch. {{right|''Pope'', Iliad, xvii. 451.}}</poem>}}
{{smaller block|And [the satyrs] ''tug'' their shaggy Beards, and bite with Grief the Ground. {{right|''Congreve'', Death of Queen Mary.}}}}
'''2.''' To tow by means of a steam-tug: as, the vessel had to be ''tugged'' into port.

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To pull with great effort; haul; drag.
{{smaller block|The meaner sort [of Dalmatians] will ''tug'' lustily at one oare.
{{right|''Sandys'', Travailes, p. 2.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
¶
'''2.''' To exert one’s self; labor; strive; struggle; contend; wrestle.

{{smaller block|<poem>The seas are rough and wider
Than his weak arms can ''tug'' with.</poem>
{{right|''Ford'', Perkin Warbeck, v. 2.}}
<poem>They ''tug'', they strain!—down, down they go,
The Gael above, Fitz-James below.</poem>
{{right|''Scott'', L. of the L., v. 16.}}}}

'''tug''' (tug), ''n.'' [< ''tug'', ''v.''; in part ult. a var. of ''tow''<sup>2</sup>, a rope, etc., and connected with ''tie''<sup>1</sup>, a band, rope, etc.; all from the ult. verb represented by ''tee''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' The act of pulling, dragging, or hauling with effort, exertion, or difficulty.

{{smaller block|<poem>The idle vessel slides that wat’ry way,
Without the blast or ''tug'' of wind or oar.</poem>
{{right|''Quarles'', Emblems, iv. 3.}}}}

'''2.''' A supreme effort; the severest strain or struggle; a contest; wrestle; tussle.

{{smaller block|She had seen from the window Tartar in full ''tug'' with two carriers’ dogs, each of them a match for him in size.
{{right|''Charlotte Brontë'', Shirley, xx.}}}}

'''3.''' A vehicle used in some parts of England for conveying timber or fagots.

{{smaller block|I have seen one tree on a carriage which they call there [in Sussex] a ''Tug'', drawn by twenty-two oxen.
{{right|''Defoe'', Tour through Great Britain, I. 204. (''Davies.'')}}}}

'''4.''' A small but powerful steam-vessel, whether screw or paddle, constructed for the purpose of towing other vessels.—'''5.''' A chain, strong rope, or leather strap used as a trace; a trace (of a harness).

{{smaller block|It [tugge] signifieth the pull or draught of the oxen or horses, and therefore the leathers that beare the chiefe}}

