'''To hold tug†''', to stand severe handling or hard work.—'''Tug of war.''' (''a'') A severe and laborious contest.
{{smaller block|When Greeks join’d Greeks, then was the ''tug of War.''
{{right|''Lee,'' Alexander the Great, iv. 2.}}}}
{{smaller block|(''b'') An athletic contest in which a number of persons, generally four on each side and limited to a certain weight, tug at the ends of a rope, each side trying to pull the rope from the other, or to pull the other side over a line marked on the ground between the contestants. Also called ''rope-pull.''}}

'''tugan,''' ''n.'' Same as ''tucan.''

'''tugboat''' (tug′bōt), ''n.'' A strongly built steamboat used for towing sailing and other vessels; a towboat; a tug.

'''tug-carrier''' (tug′kar″i-ėr), ''n.'' An attachment to the back-strap of a wagon-harness. ''E. H. Knight.''

'''tugger''' (tug′ėr), ''n.'' One who tugs, or pulls with effort.
{{smaller block|The ''tuggers'' at the oar. ''William Morris,'' Sigurd, i.}}

'''tuggingly''' (tug′ing-li), ''adv.'' With laborious pulling.
